201: ### 4.7 Persistence
202: 
203: The complete learner state is saved at every checkpoint: connection strengths, eligibility traces and any slow stores the learner keeps (stage 1's learner has a reservoir state), the diary, the reservoir counter and every random-stream state. One lesson's update, receipt and diary admission are published together in one atomic write (temporary file, flush, rename), with SHA-256 hashes in the checkpoint manifest, so a resume never has one without the others. The replay that follows is published in its own atomic write (its update, its log entry and the random-stream states) before the next lesson starts, so a resume restarts at a lesson boundary. Resume goes into a new directory after checking input hashes. Test and validation processes receive only the learner state, never the diary.
204: 
205: ### 4.8 Random streams
206: 
207: Each run seed derives separate streams. The stream seed is the first 8 bytes of the SHA-256 of the UTF-8 text `<run seed>|<label>`, read as a big-endian unsigned 64-bit integer. Labels: `tape`, `choice`, `reservoir`, `replay`, `shuffle`, `filler`, `features`. Each stream is `xoshiro256**` with its 256-bit state filled by four SplitMix64 outputs from the stream seed (the standard constructions). Uniform integer in 0 to m-1: draw 64-bit outputs, accept one only if it is below 2^64 - (2^64 mod m), and return it mod m. Uniform real in [0, 1): the top 53 bits divided by 2^53. Normal (random projection only): each normal value takes two uniform reals u1 then u2, in that order (u1 replaced by 2^-53 if it is 0), and returns V(-2 ln u1) � cos(2p u2); the sine partner is discarded, so no value is reused. The projection fills W row by row, one normal per entry, column by column within a row. Stage 0 publishes the first eight normals of the `features` stream for each registered run seed as a known-answer check; a mismatch stops the run. Permutations: Fisher-Yates driven by the uniform-integer rule. Run seeds are fixed before any run: stage 1 uses the original six seeds 4101-4106 (development data, as the original spec states); stage 2 uses 20261201, 20261202 and 20261203. One stream never feeds another's decisions, so, for example, admitting a lesson cannot change which entry is replayed next except through the diary's contents.
