290:**Contract.** Reuse the cubic-exit task's harness (`run-eye-graph-memory-cubic.mjs`; [specification](../../../research/reports/memory-retention-continuation-2026-11-01/cubic-exit-spec.md)) through an adapter. Six seeds, 4101–4106, as the original. Four items learned in sequence, each a choice between two injected, normalized Kenyon-cell codes; one unknown trial in eight; then the delay with noise; then reversal of item 0. Teaching level is the task's prediction-scaled function (`TONIC + (pulse − TONIC) × scale`), recomputed at replay from the stored valence and the present prediction. Replay is inserted after each feedback trial in every phase that has feedback; the delay has none. The task's own arms (informative, absent, shuffled, disabled) are kept; the arms of section 7.2 except mixed order, learning off (the task's disabled arm serves), case recall and the learners are added, making 12 arms. Complete state includes the task's slow store. Planning estimate: the original 24 arm runs took 6,157 s at 299 MiB peak; 12 arms × 6 seeds with replays is about 6–7 hours. A stage 1 protocol file freezes the adapter, the exact per-seed formulas below and the seed list before any run.
310:**Ideas** (from the transfer protocol): **A = legal recapture**; **B = exchange value**. A punishes recapturable captures that lose material; B rewards recapturable captures of a piece worth at least the capturer. They are consistent under the protocol's combined rule, so interference happens only if the fly's connections mix the two features. Whether they do is unknown; the no-diary arm measures it.
316:**All-legal measure.** On the 192 development twin boards of the stage (96 per idea) that have at least one verified-good legal move (boards with none are excluded before testing and counted), take the fly's top-scoring moves within the tie tolerance. The same all-move scores give the offset-cancelled pairs. Good means a capture that is good under the protocol's combined rule; a quiet move is not good. King moves and moves the checker cannot certify are unknown. The board scores the share of tied top moves that are good. A board whose tied top set contains an unknown move is an unknown board. **Every eligible board stays in the denominator.** When the diary arm is compared with another arm, the comparison uses the worst case for the diary: the diary arm's unknown boards score 0 and the other arm's unknown boards score 1. The bootstrap lower limit of that worst-case paired difference must be above 0; the version with unknown boards left out is reported beside it. Reason: leaving unknowns out separately in each arm can change the denominators and make an advantage out of nothing (90 of 183 against 90 of 192). If more than 5% of eligible boards are unknown in any arm, the measure is inconclusive and so is the stage.
333:8. **Useful choices:** all-legal measure after B above learning off, with the worst-case scoring of unknown boards above, bootstrap lower limit above 0.
337:**Worked example of the unknown-board rule.** Diary arm: 192 eligible development boards, 183 scored (90 good) and 9 unknown. Other arm: 192 scored, 90 good. Worst case for the diary: its 9 unknowns score 0, so 90 of 192 against 90 of 192: difference 0, and no advantage can pass. Left out instead, 90 of 183 (0.49) against 90 of 192 (0.47) would look like an advantage out of nothing; that version is reported beside the main result and cannot pass alone.
341:**Reading the result:** all pass → replay keeps chess idea A while B is learned, beyond extra practice, timing, re-exposure and wrong verdicts: assisted retention, Volume 3. Large sawtooth with a low-point failure → refresh, not joint storage. Mixed order succeeds but the diary fails → a joint setting is reachable and this replay schedule does not find it. Mixed order fails → this schedule failed; a Volume 4 limit needs a separate bounded proof or exact search over the permitted connection strengths, as in the revision-4 capacity checks. Capacity curve: a benefit only when the diary keeps every lesson means the design does not work under pressure.
347:Use [Phase B and Phase C of the transfer protocol](../research-goals/transfer-and-combination-protocol-2026-11-03.md) unchanged, on the fly's own choices. For each fly arm of the protocol (Phase-A, fresh, shuffled, single-idea and two-idea flies) add a version with a diary, and add the case-recall arms (diary and full history) and both learners with the diary. Each diary belongs to one fly and travels with its learner state across phases; its capacity is one quarter of that fly's total planned lessons across all its phases, fixed before data. The protocol's filler trials carry feedback the fly receives, so they are lessons and enter the diary like any other. The fly is always tested with the diary removed. Each locked set is opened once.
349:**Matched control input.** The matched tape has four fields only: the delivered stimulus, the external teaching input (or none), the event kind (lesson or replay) and the order. Replay logs (dopamine factors, update sizes, the chosen entry) are fly-internal and are not on the tape. Each control arm with a diary receives, for the fly it is compared with, the tape up to the exact receipt cutoff of that fly's selected snapshot, including whether that snapshot's last replay was applied; no event after the cutoff is given. Each control then uses the tape as follows: the final-diary lookup reads that fly's frozen diary at the cutoff; the full-history lookup has one entry per original lesson receipt (replays are never stored as cases); the simple learners apply every tape event as an update. The protocol's missing-idea and filler conditions are kept as the protocol defines them, so a control that lacks an idea never receives the omitted lessons. Fly arms choose for themselves, so they see different lessons; for the central attribution test, the learner arms are matched to the evaluated full fly's tape. The auditor checks byte equality of the matched tape for each comparison, separately from each arm's own update records.
356:4. the all-legal measure on the locked boards, scored as in stage 2 (fixed denominator, worst case for the diary on unknown boards), above learning off, the case-recall arms and both learners, each with bootstrap lower limit above 0.
367:6. **Refresh only:** the diary fails at the low-point snapshot.
